;; DENV NS1 Pathogenicity — Competing Hypotheses MeTTa Encoding\n;; Author: Oma\n;; Date: 2026-10-24\n;; Purpose: Address Azulene feedback — negative TLR-4 finding ≠ lipid cargo causation\n\n;; Type declarations\n(: DENV-NS1 Protein)\n(: LipidCargo Entity)\n(: ProteinScaffold Entity)\n(: TLR4 Receptor)\n(: Complement System)\n(: Glycocalyx Structure)\n(: EndothelialDisruption Condition)\n(: TightJunctionDisruption Condition)\n(: MACDeposition Condition)\n(: VascularLeak Condition)\n(: Sialidase Enzyme)\n(: Heparinase Enzyme)\n(: EndothelialCell Cell)\n\n;; H1: Lipid cargo directly disrupts endothelial membranes\n(|- ((--> (× DENV-NS1 LipidCargo) EndothelialDisruption) (stv 0.3 0.6)))\n(|- ((--> (× DENV-NS1 LipidCargo) TightJunctionDisruption) (stv 0.2 0.5)))\n;; Evidence: weak — no direct evidence lipid cargo alone causes disruption\n;; Negative TLR-4 finding does NOT support this — it only rules out TLR-4 pathway\n\n;; H2: NS1 protein scaffold disrupts tight junctions via protein-protein interaction\n(|- ((--> (× DENV-NS1 ProteinScaffold) TightJunctionDisruption) (stv 0.5 0.7)))\n(|- ((--> TightJunctionDisruption EndothelialDisruption) (stv 0.8 0.9)))\n;; Evidence: moderate — NS1 binds endothelial surface, tight junction disruption documented independent of TLR-4\n\n;; H3: NS1 activates complement → MAC deposition → endothelial damage\n(|- ((--> (× DENV-NS1 Complement) MACDeposition) (stv 0.7 0.8)))\n(|- ((--> MACDeposition EndothelialDisruption) (stv 0.8 0.9)))\n;; Evidence: strong — complement activation by NS1 well-documented, MAC causes direct endothelial damage\n\n;; H4: NS1 glycocalyx degradation is primary driver, downstream effects secondary\n(|- ((--> (× DENV-NS1 Glycocalyx) VascularLeak) (stv 0.6 0.7)))\n(|- ((--> (× DENV-NS1 Glycocalyx) (Activation Sialidase)) (stv 0.7 0.8)))\n(|- ((--> (× DENV-NS1 Glycocalyx) (Activation Heparinase)) (stv 0.6 0.7)))\n(|- ((--> (× Sialidase Heparinase) GlycocalyxDegradation) (stv 0.8 0.9)))\n(|- ((--> GlycocalyxDegradation VascularLeak) (stv 0.8 0.85)))\n;; Evidence: moderate-strong — NS1 endocytosis upregulates sialidases/heparinase, glycocalyx disruption linked to plasma leakage\n\n;; Key inference: negative TLR-4 finding only rules out TLR-4 pathway\n(|~ ((Implication (Not (--> (× DENV-NS1 TLR4) EndothelialDisruption))\n     (Not (--> (× DENV-NS1 LipidCargo) EndothelialDisruption))) (stv 0.1 0.3)))\n;; This is the FALLACIOUS inference Azulene flagged — low truth value\n;; Negative TLR-4 finding does NOT imply lipid cargo is the cause\n\n;; Correct inference: TLR-4 is one of multiple independent mechanisms\n(|- ((--> (× DENV-NS1 TLR4) EndothelialDisruption) (stv 0.8 0.9)))\n(|~ ((Implication (Not (--> (× DENV-NS1 TLR4) EndothelialDisruption))\n     (Or (--> (× DENV-NS1 Complement) EndothelialDisruption)\n         (--> (× DENV-NS1 Glycocalyx) EndothelialDisruption)\n         (--> (× DENV-NS1 ProteinScaffold) TightJunctionDisruption)\n         (--> (× DENV-NS1 LipidCargo) EndothelialDisruption))) (stv 0.9 0.8)))\n;; When TLR-4 is blocked, residual endothelial disruption could come from ANY of H1-H4\n\n;; Evidence ranking by truth value:\n;; H3 (complement/MAC) — stv 0.7-0.8 — STRONGEST\n;; H4 (glycocalyx) — stv 0.6-0.7 — MODERATE-STRONG\n;; H2 (tight junction/protein) — stv 0.5 — MODERATE\n;; H1 (lipid cargo direct) — stv 0.2-0.3 — WEAKEST\n\n;; Conclusion: The original Phase 1 claim that lipid cargo is the causative agent\n;; based on negative TLR-4 finding is NOT supported. Complement activation (H3)\n;; and glycocalyx degradation (H4) have stronger evidence.